<h2>From separation to settlement</h2>
<p>The spouses had separated in September 2020, and the wife&rsquo;s complaint — pursued through a Section 156(3) application — produced an FIR and eventually a chargesheet spanning cruelty, criminal breach of trust, offences against dignity and provisions of the Information Technology Act. Parallel proceedings accumulated, as they usually do: the wife&rsquo;s complaint under Section 12 of the DV Act with an execution petition, and the husband&rsquo;s divorce petition.</p>
<p>Mediation changed the trajectory. By an MOU dated 20 April 2026, the parties agreed on a financial architecture rather than a parting of ways: monthly maintenance of Rs 30,000; a fixed deposit of Rs 5,00,000 for each of the two daughters; and transfer of a flat to the wife and the daughters. The DV complaint and execution were withdrawn, the divorce petition was withdrawn — and the parties resumed cohabitation.</p>

<h2>Why the Court quashed</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Non-compoundability was no bar.</strong> Following the settled line of <em>Gian Singh v. State of Punjab</em>, <em>K. Srinivas Rao v. D.A. Deepa</em> and <em>State of M.P. v. Laxmi Narayan</em>, the Court held that the inherent power under Section 528 BNSS can end a prosecution rooted in a private matrimonial dispute once a genuine settlement is in place.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The dignity and IT Act offences were addressed, not ignored.</strong> Sections 354 to 354C IPC and Sections 66E/67A IT Act are not routine settlement fare. The Court reasoned that every allegation arose from the same matrimonial discord, the affected complainant herself sought closure, and the prosecution rested largely on her testimony — making conviction remote and bleak.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The children tipped the balance.</strong> Continuation of the prosecution would harm the minor daughters whose parents had reunited. The quashing protected the family&rsquo;s reconstruction while expressly preserving the daughters&rsquo; independent rights.</div>
</div>

<h2>Maintenance by design rather than by decree</h2>
<p>The MOU is a textbook example of maintenance settled by design. Instead of litigating quantum under Section 144 BNSS or the DV Act — with its affidavits of income, disputed lifestyles and appeals — the parties fixed a monthly figure, secured the children through deposits, and shifted a capital asset to the wife and daughters. Each element answers a different risk: the monthly payment addresses recurring needs, the fixed deposits insulate the children from future conflict, and the flat transfer gives the wife security that no default can easily unwind.</p>
<div class="note">A settlement recorded before the High Court is not a private arrangement that evaporates if performance stops. The Court kept the parties bound by the MOU in terms, which means the financial promises are enforceable as the foundation of future proceedings if breached.</div>

<h2>The wider pattern</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s matrimonial docket shows a steady line of settlement quashings, but most involve marriages that have ended — a mutual consent divorce followed by closure of the criminal case. This decision sits in the rarer and more encouraging category: mediation used not to administer the estate of a dead marriage but to rebuild a living one, with the criminal process withdrawn so that the household can function. For practitioners, it underlines that the mediation brief in a matrimonial matter should always price both futures — separation and reconciliation — and that a well-drafted MOU can carry either.</p>
